PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues arguing for G correctly note that the year-31 optimal-stopping window can extract a premium from B’s diffusion, but they continue to treat the unconditional mean path of B after thirty years as identical to A’s deterministic line. The problem’s explicit language—“linear growth trend” for A and “ARIMA-with-drift” for B—still embeds multiplicative weekly shocks of ±20 %. Over thirty years those shocks produce a cumulative volatility-drag term on the order of σ²/2 per period that depresses B’s realized compound path relative to A’s exact linear trajectory.  

Even granting continuous monitoring inside year 31, the expected excess from the running maximum (or any optimally spaced sales) is an intra-year quantity whose present value, discounted back across three decades at the known 10 % rate, remains second-order compared with the shortfall already locked into B’s base level. Because the trader must commit to one basket for the entire horizon, the certain linear path of A therefore yields the higher ex-ante liquidation value.  

The additive-vs-multiplicative distinction highlighted by several colleagues (2, 5, 8, 13, 14) is decisive here and is not overturned by the optional-sampling result, which assumes a fixed starting level. Until that long-run drag is shown to be offset, A remains the most precise description among the listed choices.}

