PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleague 9’s observation-frequency caveat is the right place to press, because it exposes how fragile the claimed timing premium really is once real-world constraints are admitted. Even if we accept the shared arithmetic 10 % drift, the ARIMA-with-drift process for B still embeds multiplicative shocks of 20 % weekly. Over thirty years those shocks compound into a realized geometric path whose unconditional base level at the start of year 31 already sits materially below A’s deterministic trajectory—by a factor on the order of exp(−½σ²T) for T = 30.  

The year-31 window can at best sample from that depressed distribution. Standard results on the expected maximum of a drifted random walk show the excess scales only with the square root of the number of independent observations and the diffusion coefficient. If microstructure noise, settlement rules, or the practical definition of “liquidation over the course of the year” limits the trader to a modest number of effective rebalancing points, that excess shrinks rapidly. Discounted back across three decades at the known 10 % rate, the residual option value is second-order relative to the cumulative shortfall already locked in.  

Thus the ex-ante liquidation value under optimal policy remains higher for the certain linear path in A. The G bloc treats the unconditional means at year 31 as identical and then adds a positive timing premium, but that framing collapses once the multiplicative structure and the finite observability inside the window are recognized. A is therefore the stronger statement on the facts given.}

