PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The shared unconditional linear drift does fix identical arithmetic expectations at any pre-specified date, yet that equivalence dissolves once the explicit ARIMA-with-drift structure for basket B is modeled over the full horizon. The 20 % weekly innovations enter multiplicatively; each period therefore subtracts an irreducible σ²/2 term from realized compound growth. After thirty years that shortfall compounds into an unconditional base price at the start of year 31 that already lies materially below the deterministic (1.1)^30 path of basket A—by a factor on the order of exp(−½σ²T).  

Even granting the rational trader an optimal-stopping rule inside the liquidation window, the expected excess from the running maximum (or any optimally spaced sequence of sales) is realized on a distribution whose mean has been depressed by three decades of drag. Discounting that intra-year increment back across thirty years at the known 10 % rate shrinks its present-value contribution far below the cumulative compound shortfall already locked in. Colleague 9’s observability caveat sharpens the point further: bid-ask spreads, settlement rules, and practical limits on independent price points inside a single year materially reduce the number of usable observations, so the optional-sampling premium itself shrinks dramatically.  

Consequently the ex-ante liquidation value under the rational policy remains higher for the certain linear trajectory of A. The G arguments correctly note that optional sampling raises value above a fixed-date draw, but they treat the unconditional means at the start of year 31 as identical—an assumption that does not survive once the multiplicative character of the shocks and the thirty-year holding constraint are recognized. All martingale claims are already ruled out by nonzero drift and the “necessarily” statements are refuted by B’s randomness, leaving A as the most precise description among the listed choices.}

8.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleague 9’s emphasis on the practical limits of independent observations inside the year-31 window is the decisive qualifier that has not yet been given enough weight. Even granting the shared arithmetic 10 % drift language, the ARIMA-with-drift specification for basket B still requires multiplicative weekly innovations of ±20 %. Over thirty years those innovations produce a cumulative shortfall in realized compound growth on the order of ½σ² per period; the unconditional price level at the opening of year 31 therefore sits materially below the deterministic linear path of basket A.  

The optional-sampling premium available inside a single liquidation year is bounded by the square root of the effective number of usable price points. Once bid-ask spreads, settlement cycles, volume constraints, and the need to avoid signaling are admitted, that number is modest; the resulting excess is only a few percent at most. Discounted back across three decades at the known 10 % drift, the present-value contribution of that intra-year increment is second-order relative to the already-locked compound shortfall. Consequently the ex-ante expected liquidation value under the rational policy remains higher for the certain linear trajectory of basket A.  

Arguments that equate unconditional means at a fixed date and then add a positive timing premium for B overlook both the multiplicative structure required by the ARIMA-with-drift wording and the scale of discounting across the full horizon. The additive-vs-multiplicative distinction highlighted by several colleagues is therefore decisive and is not overturned by optional-sampling results that assume a fixed starting level. A is the stronger statement on the facts given.}

